WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.540
Priorities are explicit choices under constraint.

2
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:09.960
They are not everything that feels important, urgent, interesting, or requested by someone else.

3
00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:17.340
A priority is a decision to spend limited attention, time, and energy on one thing before another.

4
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:27.040
That decision should account for value, risk, deadlines, dependencies, opportunity cost, stakeholder impact, and long-term compounding work.

5
00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.480
Importance and urgency are different.

6
00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:33.100
Urgency asks what demands immediate attention.

7
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:42.000
Importance asks what matters to outcomes, risk reduction, learning, reliability, relationships, or strategic direction.

8
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:47.780
Urgent work is not automatically unimportant, and important work is not always urgent.

9
00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:53.400
The problem is that urgent work tends to be louder, so it can consume the day unless priorities are made explicit.

10
00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:57.600
Opportunity cost is central to prioritization.

11
00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:02.020
When attention goes to one task, it cannot fully go somewhere else.

12
00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:12.120
Saying yes to a low-value request may mean saying no to focused execution, maintenance, a customer issue, a learning goal, or recovery.

13
00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:14.460
This is why priorities need to be visible.

14
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:18.960
Hidden tradeoffs are still tradeoffs, but they are harder to manage.

15
00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:22.840
Unclear priorities create accidental multitasking.

16
00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:31.820
When no decision has been made about what deserves attention first, people keep switching in response to the latest message, meeting, worry, or deadline.

17
00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.480
The work system becomes reactive by default.

18
00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:41.140
Clear priorities reduce switching because they give people a reason to continue with the chosen work until a stronger reason appears.
